<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Carnonacae</h1><p class="st-meta">100 CE – 200 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">People of north-west Scotland named in Ptolemy's Geography, usually placed on the coast of Wester Ross.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Carnonacae appear in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geography-ptolemy">Ptolemy's Geography</a> of about 150 on the western coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scottish-highlands">Scottish Highlands</a>, north of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/creones">Creones</a> and south of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/caereni">Caereni</a>, roughly in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wester-ross">Wester Ross</a>. Their name may contain a Celtic element meaning cairn or horn, perhaps referring to rocky headlands.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Nothing is recorded of their rulers or institutions. The coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wester-ross">Wester Ross</a> supported small populations living in roundhouses and duns, with brochs appearing along the Atlantic seaboard in the last centuries BCE. These tall drystone towers suggest local elites able to organise considerable labour.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Carnonacae do not appear in any later source. Their territory later formed part of the lands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a> and, from the 9th century, came under strong Norse influence.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/carnonacae.jpg" alt="Carnonacae" width="320" height="464"><figcaption>Image: (myself) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Britain.north.peoples.Ptolemy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>100 CE – 200 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Celtic polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Common Brittonic (Pictish)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>United Kingdom</dd></dl></aside></div>

